UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 27, Lesson 1: Estimating measurements', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nEstimate the capacity of a classroom bin and the mass of a pair of shoes. Are your estimates sensible?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.\n\nWATCH FOR\nWild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake twelve measurement estimates (length/mass/capacity). Mark each sensible/unsensible with a benchmark reason.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Concept","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-NSM-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make twelve measurement estimates (length/mass/capacity). Mark each sensible/unsensible with a benchmark reason. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates. Reasoning: I used estimate by attending to measurement rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Correction: Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates. Why: the correction restores accurate use of estimate.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses estimate with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with estimate, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates. This matches the lesson skill for estimate.","options":["Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.","Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense."],"question":"Estimate the capacity of a classroom bin and the mass of a pair of shoes. Are your estimates sensible?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense.","Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness.","Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.","Careful use of measurement with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named measurement, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the measurement used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"A learner is practising estimate. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense.","Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.","Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness. — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for estimate.","Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses estimate with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates. Reasoning: I used estimate by attending to measurement rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with estimate, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Correction: Bin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates. Why: the correction restores accurate use of estimate.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w27-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise estimate.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use estimate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify measurement and explain why it matters.","I can practise benchmark independently on a new prompt.","I can notice check needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEstimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable.\n\nTRY FIRST\nEstimate the capacity of a classroom bin and the mass of a pair of shoes. Are your estimates sensible?\n\nFEEDBACK\nBin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.","explain":"Estimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Estimate with benchmarks.","caption":"Flag wild guesses.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Benchmark</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bottle ≈1 L</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sensible?</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Estimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Estimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no working.","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEstimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Sensible estimate vs wild guess.","caption":"Twelve estimates.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Book ~500 g</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">not 50 kg</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">no benchmark</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Here is a more productive model: Estimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBin often tens of litres; shoes often hundreds of grams — accept reasoned sensible estimates.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Wild guesses without a benchmark cannot be checked for sense. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEstimating measurements uses known benchmarks, then a measure or conversion check tests closeness. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 27, Lesson 1: Estimating measurements","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Volume, capacity and mass","week":27,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nEstimate a full water bottle ≈ 1 L; a textbook ≈ 500 g. An estimate of 50 kg for a book is unreasonable.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Estimate with benchmarks.","caption":"Flag wild guesses.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Benchmark</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">bottle ≈1 L</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Check</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">sensible?</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Sensible estimate vs wild guess.","caption":"Twelve estimates.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"3